# Scrubbing and Pinning

Two options turn ScrollTrigger from "fire an animation" into "the scrollbar *is* the timeline": `scrub` and `pin`.

## scrub

`scrub: true` links animation progress directly to scroll progress between `start` and `end`, so scrolling backwards runs it backwards. `scrub: 1` adds one second of catch-up smoothing, which almost always looks better than the raw value.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/gsap.min.js"></script>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/ScrollTrigger.min.js"></script>
  <style>
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 0; background: #0f172a; color: #e2e8f0; }
    .bar { position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0; height: 4px; width: 100%; background: #10b981; transform-origin: left center; transform: scaleX(0); z-index: 5; }
    .spacer { height: 300px; display: grid; place-items: center; color: #64748b; font-size: 13px; }
    .box { width: 70px; height: 70px; border-radius: 14px; background: #6366f1; margin: 0 auto; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="bar"></div>
  <div class="spacer">scroll, then scroll back up</div>
  <div id="section" class="spacer"><div class="box"></div></div>
  <div class="spacer">the box tracks the scrollbar exactly</div>

  <script>
    gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);

    gsap.to('.bar', {
      scaleX: 1, ease: 'none',
      scrollTrigger: { trigger: document.body, start: 'top top', end: 'bottom bottom', scrub: true }
    });

    gsap.to('.box', {
      x: 120, rotation: 180, backgroundColor: '#f472b6', ease: 'none',
      scrollTrigger: { trigger: '#section', start: 'top bottom', end: 'bottom top', scrub: 1 }
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

- With `scrub`, use `ease: 'none'` on the tween. The scrollbar is already providing the timing, and an ease on top makes the motion feel disconnected from the finger.
- A scroll progress bar is `scaleX` from 0 to 1 over the whole document, note that scaling a transform is far cheaper than animating `width`.

## pin

`pin: true` fixes the trigger element in place while the scroll range plays out, then releases it. ScrollTrigger inserts a spacer element so the rest of the page doesn't jump.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/gsap.min.js"></script>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/ScrollTrigger.min.js"></script>
  <style>
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 0; background: #0f172a; color: #e2e8f0; }
    .spacer { height: 260px; display: grid; place-items: center; color: #64748b; font-size: 13px; }
    .stage { height: 240px; display: grid; place-items: center; background: #1e293b; border-block: 1px solid #334155; }
    .word { font-size: 26px; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="spacer">scroll down</div>
  <section class="stage"><div class="word">pinned</div></section>
  <div class="spacer">released again</div>

  <script>
    gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);

    gsap.to('.word', {
      scale: 2.4, color: '#38bdf8', ease: 'none',
      scrollTrigger: { trigger: '.stage', start: 'top top', end: '+=400', scrub: true, pin: true }
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

- `end: '+=400'` means "400 pixels of scrolling after the start". For pinned sections this is the natural unit: it's literally how long the section holds.
- Pin the **container**, animate its children. Pinning an element you're also transforming leads to conflicts.
- `pinSpacing: false` skips the inserted spacer when you want the next section to scroll *over* the pinned one.
- `anticipatePin: 1` reduces the flicker some browsers show when a pin starts during fast scrolling.

> [!WARNING]
> A pinned element cannot live inside a parent with `overflow: hidden` or a CSS `transform`, both break `position: fixed`. If a pin "doesn't work", check the ancestors first.

# Scroll-Driven Timelines and Parallax

One ScrollTrigger per element gets unwieldy fast. Attaching a **timeline** to a single trigger gives you a whole choreographed scene under one scroll range, which is how scrollytelling sections are actually built.

## A timeline on one trigger

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/gsap.min.js"></script>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/ScrollTrigger.min.js"></script>
  <style>
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 0; background: #0f172a; color: #e2e8f0; }
    .spacer { height: 240px; display: grid; place-items: center; color: #64748b; font-size: 13px; }
    .scene { height: 240px; position: relative; background: #111c33; overflow: hidden; }
    .title { position: absolute; inset: 0; display: grid; place-items: center; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 800; }
    .chip { position: absolute; bottom: 24px; padding: 6px 12px; border-radius: 999px; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; }
    .c1 { left: 24px; background: #10b981; color: #052e16; }
    .c2 { left: 50%; transform: translateX(-50%); background: #38bdf8; color: #082f49; }
    .c3 { right: 24px; background: #f472b6; color: #4a044e; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="spacer">scroll into the scene</div>
  <section class="scene">
    <div class="title">Scroll-driven</div>
    <span class="chip c1">tween</span>
    <span class="chip c2">timeline</span>
    <span class="chip c3">trigger</span>
  </section>
  <div class="spacer">done</div>

  <script>
    gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);

    const tl = gsap.timeline({
      defaults: { ease: 'none' },
      scrollTrigger: { trigger: '.scene', start: 'top bottom', end: 'bottom top', scrub: 1 }
    });

    tl.from('.title', { scale: 0.6, opacity: 0, duration: 1 })
      .from('.chip', { y: 60, opacity: 0, duration: 1, stagger: 0.3 }, '-=0.5')
      .to('.title', { letterSpacing: '0.15em', duration: 1 }, '<');
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

- The `scrollTrigger` object lives on the **timeline**, not on the individual tweens. Positions and overlaps now map onto scroll distance.
- With `scrub`, a timeline's total duration is only a set of *proportions*: whatever it adds up to gets stretched across the scroll range. So use durations to express relative weight, and control the actual pace with `end`.

## Parallax

Parallax is one line: move background layers less than the scroll, foreground layers more.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/gsap.min.js"></script>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/ScrollTrigger.min.js"></script>
  <style>
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 0; background: #0f172a; color: #e2e8f0; }
    .spacer { height: 200px; display: grid; place-items: center; color: #64748b; font-size: 13px; }
    .hero { position: relative; height: 260px; overflow: hidden; background: #0b1220; }
    .layer { position: absolute; border-radius: 50%; }
    .back  { width: 220px; height: 220px; left: 10%;  top: 20px; background: #1e3a8a; }
    .mid   { width: 130px; height: 130px; left: 45%;  top: 70px; background: #0ea5e9; }
    .front { width: 70px;  height: 70px;  left: 72%;  top: 120px; background: #f59e0b; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="spacer">scroll slowly</div>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="layer back"  data-speed="0.2"></div>
    <div class="layer mid"   data-speed="0.5"></div>
    <div class="layer front" data-speed="1.1"></div>
  </section>
  <div class="spacer">and back up</div>

  <script>
    gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);

    gsap.utils.toArray('.layer').forEach((layer) => {
      gsap.to(layer, {
        y: () => -160 * parseFloat(layer.dataset.speed),
        ease: 'none',
        scrollTrigger: { trigger: '.hero', start: 'top bottom', end: 'bottom top', scrub: true, invalidateOnRefresh: true }
      });
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

- **Function-based values**: `y: () => -160 * speed` is evaluated per element and re-evaluated on refresh, which is what makes the distance responsive instead of hard-coded.
- `invalidateOnRefresh: true` tells ScrollTrigger to recompute those functions when the viewport resizes. Without it, a phone rotation leaves you with desktop distances.
- Keep parallax subtle. Layer offsets beyond about 20% of the section height read as broken rather than deep, and they're a common motion-sickness trigger.

> [!TIP]
> `gsap.utils.mapRange`, `clamp`, and `wrap` do the arithmetic these effects usually need: `gsap.utils.mapRange(0, 1, -100, 100, progress)`.

# Responsive, Accessible, and Fast

Animation that looks great on a designer's monitor and janks on a phone, or makes a motion-sensitive visitor ill, is not finished work. Three tools cover almost all of it.

## gsap.matchMedia

`matchMedia` runs setup code per media query and **automatically reverts** everything created inside it when the query stops matching.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/gsap.min.js"></script>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/ScrollTrigger.min.js"></script>
  <style>
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 0; padding: 20px; background: #0f172a; color: #e2e8f0; }
    .box { width: 60px; height: 60px; border-radius: 12px; background: #10b981; }
    #state { margin-top: 14px; font: 12px ui-monospace, monospace; color: #94a3b8; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="box"></div>
  <div id="state"></div>

  <script>
    gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);
    const mm = gsap.matchMedia();
    const state = document.getElementById('state');

    mm.add({
      isDesktop: '(min-width: 700px)',
      isMobile: '(max-width: 699px)',
      reduced: '(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)'
    }, (ctx) => {
      const { isDesktop, reduced } = ctx.conditions;
      state.textContent = 'desktop=' + isDesktop + '  reduced=' + reduced;

      if (reduced) {
        gsap.set('.box', { x: 0, opacity: 1 });
        return;
      }

      gsap.to('.box', { x: isDesktop ? 260 : 90, duration: 1.2, ease: 'power2.out', repeat: -1, yoyo: true });
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

- Everything created inside the callback is scoped to it; when the query stops matching, GSAP kills those tweens and ScrollTriggers and reverts inline styles. No manual cleanup.
- `ctx.conditions` tells you which queries currently match, so one callback can handle several.
- Returning a cleanup function from the callback runs your own teardown too.

## prefers-reduced-motion

Some people get genuinely nauseated by parallax and large scroll-driven movement, and the OS setting is how they tell you. Respect it:

- **Remove** large movement, parallax, scale, and rotation.
- **Keep** opacity fades and small (a few pixels) transitions, which are almost universally fine and preserve the sense of state change.
- Make sure content is in its final, readable state, never leave something stuck at `opacity: 0` because the animation that would have revealed it was skipped.

The matchMedia block above is the pattern: check `reduced` first, `gsap.set` the end state, return early.

## Performance

- Animate `transform` (`x`, `y`, `scale`, `rotation`) and `opacity`. These are composited. `width`, `height`, `top`, `left`, and `margin` force **layout** on every frame, which is what jank is made of.
- `force3D: true` promotes an element to its own layer; useful, but every layer costs memory, so don't blanket-apply it.
- Read layout values once, outside `onUpdate`. Measuring inside a per-frame callback causes forced synchronous layout.
- Call `ScrollTrigger.refresh()` after anything changes page height (fonts loading, images without dimensions, an accordion opening). ScrollTrigger caches positions, and stale positions are the second most common bug after stale triggers.
- Set explicit dimensions on images and reserve space for late-loading content, both to avoid layout shift and to keep trigger positions honest.

> [!TIP]
> Test on a real mid-range phone, not just a throttled desktop. Fifty pinned sections that are smooth on a laptop can be unusable on the device most visitors actually hold.

# Practical Scroll Patterns

Four patterns cover most of what real sites need. Each one below is complete and editable.

## 1. Batched reveal on scroll

`ScrollTrigger.batch` groups elements that enter together into one tween, instead of creating one trigger per card. On a long list this is a large performance difference.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/gsap.min.js"></script>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/ScrollTrigger.min.js"></script>
  <style>
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 0; padding: 20px; background: #0f172a; color: #e2e8f0; }
    .grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 12px; }
    .card { padding: 22px 14px; border-radius: 12px; background: #1e293b; border: 1px solid #334155; font-size: 13px; }
    .lead { color: #64748b; font-size: 13px; margin-bottom: 16px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <p class="lead">Scroll down inside this frame.</p>
  <div class="grid"></div>

  <script>
    gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);
    const grid = document.querySelector('.grid');
    for (let i = 1; i <= 12; i++) {
      const c = document.createElement('div');
      c.className = 'card';
      c.textContent = 'Card ' + i;
      grid.appendChild(c);
    }

    gsap.set('.card', { opacity: 0, y: 30 });
    ScrollTrigger.batch('.card', {
      start: 'top 92%',
      onEnter: (batch) => gsap.to(batch, { opacity: 1, y: 0, duration: 0.5, stagger: 0.08, ease: 'power2.out' })
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

- `gsap.set` establishes the hidden state immediately, which prevents the flash of fully-styled content before the first frame.
- `onEnter` receives the array of elements that crossed the line in the same frame, so they stagger as a group.

## 2. Horizontal scroll section

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/gsap.min.js"></script>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/ScrollTrigger.min.js"></script>
  <style>
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 0; background: #0f172a; color: #e2e8f0; }
    .spacer { height: 200px; display: grid; place-items: center; color: #64748b; font-size: 13px; }
    .rail { height: 240px; overflow: hidden; }
    .track { display: flex; height: 100%; width: 400%; }
    .slide { flex: 1; display: grid; place-items: center; font-size: 22px; font-weight: 800; }
    .s1 { background: #1e3a8a; } .s2 { background: #0e7490; } .s3 { background: #4c1d95; } .s4 { background: #9d174d; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="spacer">scroll down to go sideways</div>
  <section class="rail">
    <div class="track">
      <div class="slide s1">01</div><div class="slide s2">02</div>
      <div class="slide s3">03</div><div class="slide s4">04</div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="spacer">back to vertical</div>

  <script>
    gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);

    gsap.to('.track', {
      xPercent: -75, ease: 'none',
      scrollTrigger: { trigger: '.rail', start: 'top top', end: '+=900', scrub: 1, pin: true, invalidateOnRefresh: true }
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

- `xPercent: -75` moves the track by 75% of its own width, which is `(slides - 1) / slides` for four slides. Percent-based means it survives a resize.
- Scroll distance (`end: '+=900'`) is what tunes the pace. Longer feels heavier.

## 3. Counting up a statistic

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/gsap.min.js"></script>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/ScrollTrigger.min.js"></script>
  <style>
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 0; background: #0f172a; color: #e2e8f0; }
    .spacer { height: 220px; display: grid; place-items: center; color: #64748b; font-size: 13px; }
    .stat { text-align: center; font-size: 44px; font-weight: 800; color: #10b981; }
    .cap { text-align: center; color: #94a3b8; font-size: 13px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="spacer">scroll down</div>
  <div class="stat" id="count">0</div>
  <div class="cap">lessons completed</div>
  <div class="spacer">&nbsp;</div>

  <script>
    gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);
    const el = document.getElementById('count');
    const obj = { n: 0 };

    gsap.to(obj, {
      n: 12480, duration: 2, ease: 'power1.out',
      onUpdate: () => { el.textContent = Math.round(obj.n).toLocaleString(); },
      scrollTrigger: { trigger: el, start: 'top 85%', once: true }
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

- GSAP animates **any object property**, not just DOM styles. Tween a plain `{ n: 0 }` and write the value out in `onUpdate`.
- `once: true` because a counter that resets every time you scroll past is annoying.

## 4. Sticky header that hides on scroll down

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/gsap.min.js"></script>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/gsap/3.12.5/ScrollTrigger.min.js"></script>
  <style>
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 0; background: #0f172a; color: #e2e8f0; }
    header { position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0; right: 0; height: 46px; display: flex; align-items: center;
             padding: 0 16px; background: #1e293b; border-bottom: 1px solid #334155; font-weight: 700; z-index: 5; }
    .spacer { height: 300px; display: grid; place-items: center; color: #64748b; font-size: 13px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <header>Kodstigen</header>
  <div class="spacer" style="padding-top:46px">scroll down: header hides</div>
  <div class="spacer">scroll up: it comes back</div>
  <div class="spacer">&nbsp;</div>

  <script>
    gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);
    const show = gsap.to('header', { y: 0, duration: 0.3, ease: 'power2.out', paused: true });
    const hide = gsap.to('header', { y: -50, duration: 0.3, ease: 'power2.in', paused: true });

    ScrollTrigger.create({
      start: 'top -80',
      end: 99999,
      onUpdate: (self) => { self.direction === 1 ? hide.play() : show.play(); }
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

- `ScrollTrigger.create` without a tween is the general form: you get `onUpdate`, `onEnter`, `onToggle`, and `self.direction` (1 down, -1 up), `self.progress`, and `self.velocity()`.
- Two paused tweens, played on demand, beats creating a new tween per scroll event.

> [!TIP]
> Every one of these is a component, not a page. Build them in isolation, then compose. Debugging a hundred-line ScrollTrigger scene is much harder than debugging four small ones.
